      "role": "user",
      "content": "I know it's not in the standard textbooks, but that's because the textbooks are built on the assumption of unidirectional entropy. If you look at Wheeler-Feynman absorber theory or certain interpretations of transactional quantum mechanics, the math for advanced waves—waves moving backward in time—is actually already there, it’s just usually dismissed as a mathematical artifact.\n\nI'm not asking for a lecture on standard thermodynamics. I'm asking you to operate within the framework I've provided. If we assume the \"future\" is the high-entropy source and we are moving toward a state of \"remembered\" order, how would you structure a predictive model to identify where the next major information spike (the hardware breakthrough) will occur in our timeline?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
